<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hezbollah</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1982">1982</a>–</span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Hizbullah</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hezbollah is a Lebanese Shia Islamist political party and armed group founded in 1982 with Iranian support, in response to the Israeli invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lebanon">Lebanon</a>. It fought Israel in southern Lebanon until the Israeli withdrawal in 2000 and in the wars of 2006 and 2024, and it also intervened in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syrian-civil-war">Syrian civil war</a>. It holds seats in parliament and runs social services, while many states list it as a terrorist organisation.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Hezbollah was conceived in 1982 by Lebanese clerics and funded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, primarily to fight the Israeli invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lebanon">Lebanon</a>. The Israeli invasions of 1978 and 1982 had created a humanitarian crisis: many villages in the south were destroyed and large numbers of Shia were displaced. The Shia had also long been underrepresented in Lebanese politics, and both factors bred resentment among them. The movement was set up by local Shia committees under the leadership of Iran's first supreme leader, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ruhollah-khomeini">Ruhollah Khomeini</a>, and its forces were trained and organised by about 1,500 Iranian Revolutionary Guards, who reached a base in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beqaa-valley">Beqaa Valley</a> with the permission of the Syrian government.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In the 1980s Hezbollah emerged in South Lebanon during a consolidation of Shia militias, as a rival to the older Amal Movement. It opposed US forces in 1982–83 and fought Amal and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> in the War of the Camps of 1985–88. Its manifesto of 16 February 1985, issued by Sheikh Ibrahim al-Amin, named the struggle against what it called US and Israeli imperialism as its first aim, called for the destruction of Israel, and looked to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>'s revolution. Several attacks of 1983 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beirut">Beirut</a>, among them the US Embassy bombing and the barracks bombing, were attributed to it by some Western intelligence agencies; Hezbollah denied responsibility, and some scholars doubt it existed in that form yet.</p><p>Through the 1980s and 1990s Hezbollah waged a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> campaign against Israeli forces and the South Lebanon Army, and Israel withdrew from southern Lebanon in 2000. In the 1990s it turned from a revolutionary group into a political one, a process called its Lebanonisation. It fought in the 2006 Lebanon War and later alongside the government of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bashar-al-assad">Bashar al-Assad</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syrian-civil-war">Syrian civil war</a>. A new manifesto of 30 November 2009 opposed political sectarianism and favoured a national unity government. From October 2023 it fought a war with Israel, which brought a ceasefire in November 2024.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>Hezbollah follows the doctrine of the guardianship of the Islamic jurists developed by Khomeini, and its formal structure concentrates authority in its religious leaders. Its military branch is the Jihad Council, which includes the Islamic Resistance, and its parliamentary bloc is the Loyalty to the Resistance Bloc. It runs an extensive social programme of schools, hospitals and reconstruction projects, and since 1991 it has run the satellite station Al-Manar. Its support is concentrated among the Shia in the south, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beirut">Beirut</a> and its surroundings, and in the northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beqaa-valley">Beqaa Valley</a> and Hermel region. Some call it a resistance movement and others a terrorist group, and many states list it as the latter.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Hezbollah” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Israel held a border zone through the South Lebanon Army until May 2000, and <b>Hezbollah</b>, founded by Shia clerics trained and funded by Iran in the early 1980s, fought it.</blockquote><a href="/empire/lebanese-republic">Lebanese Republic · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Consolidating power, it executed more than 3,400 political dissidents between June 1981 and March 1982; it later brought almost universal access to electricity and clean water, and it created or assisted like-minded armed Shia groups, SCIRI in Iraq and <b>Hezbollah</b> in Lebanon.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/islamism">Islamism · In Power</a></li></ul></section>
